        "eyebrow": "Virtual staging near you",
        "heading": "Virtual Staging Boston, MA",
        "lede": "Virtual staging is a digital marketing technique for real estate — home staging done in the photo: an interior designer adds realistic furniture and decor to photos of empty or cleared rooms so potential buyers can connect emotionally with the space, without a moving truck or a rental contract. Brownstone parlors, tight condo layouts and historic rooms are exactly where staging earns its keep — Bella's designers stage Boston listings in 24 to 48 hours, from {price:virtual-staging} a photo.",
        "cta_label": "Stage my listing — from {price:virtual-staging}",
        "cta_link": "/products/virtual-staging",
        "alt_label": "Browse the lookbook",
        "alt_link": "/pages/lookbook",
        "city": "Boston",
        "market_heading": "Why virtual staging matters in the Boston market",
        "market_text": "<p>Boston's inventory is old, and old rooms confuse buyers: parlor-level living rooms with fireplaces at odd angles, third-floor walk-ups with sloped ceilings, basement units that need proof they can feel bright. Home staging answers the question every Boston buyer asks — will my furniture fit? — before they book the showing.</p><p>In a market where inventory is chronically tight and well-presented listings still draw multiple offers, the listing that photographs best sets the pace for the open house.</p><p>Boston also runs on a calendar almost no other US market shares. The September 1 lease turnover concentrates an enormous share of moves into a few weeks, which pulls listing activity forward into a crowded early-summer window. Photos that are ready when the listing goes live — not a week later once a stager's truck is scheduled — are the difference between catching that window and missing it.</p><p>97% of buyers use the internet in their home search (NAR), so for most Boston home sellers the listing photos are the first open house. Empty rooms photograph smaller than they are, and a period living room with no furniture gives buyers no sense of scale; staged photos tend to draw more clicks, views and showing requests. Virtually staged photos turn awkward rooms into functional spaces and point buyers to the key features — which is why real estate agents from Back Bay to Newton use virtual staging on luxury listings and new construction alike.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Boston home staging, done virtually for each buyer",
        "local_text": "<p>Our designers stage to the neighbourhood: classic-contemporary blends for Back Bay and Beacon Hill, cleaner modern looks for Seaport condos, and family staging for Brookline, Newton and the suburbs.</p><p>Period rooms get particular care. A Beacon Hill parlor with original mouldings and a working fireplace should look furnished, not redecorated — the staging works around the architecture buyers are paying for rather than covering it.</p><p>Our design team works online, so there's no consultation visit to arrange: order, upload your photos and a designer handles the style, art and decor. Luxury properties get a restrained palette of neutral colors and classic pieces; condos and new construction get clean modern furniture, and the design can flex to suit a young professional, a downsizer or a growing family. Every image is MLS PIN-ready with disclosure guidance, and volume discounts apply automatically — 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+ — for developers and busy teams.</p>",
        "hoods_label": "Where we stage most",
        "hoods": "Back Bay, Beacon Hill, South End, Seaport, Charlestown, Jamaica Plain, Brookline, Newton, Somerville"
      }
    },
