            "answer": "No, and it's the most common Boston request we get. Odd fireplace placement, sloped third-floor ceilings and narrow parlor proportions are worked around by hand — a designer composes each room rather than dropping in a template."
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does it cost to do virtual staging?",
            "answer": "Bella virtual staging is {price:virtual-staging} per photo, with volume discounts of 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+. Furniture removal, unlimited revisions for two weeks and full usage rights are included — no subscription and no minimum."
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much do home staging services cost in Boston?",
            "answer": "Physical staging is priced by the stager, the size of the home and how long the furniture stays, so Boston quotes vary; as a general benchmark it runs about $2,000–5,000 a month. Virtual staging is {price:virtual-staging} a photo, once, with no rental period to extend."
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging worth it?",
            "answer": "For most vacant Boston listings, yes. Staging can't guarantee a sale, but 83% of buyers' agents say staging makes it easier for a buyer to visualize the property as a future home (NAR, 2025), and 97% of buyers use the internet in their home search, so the photos are the first showing. Virtual staging furnishes them for a one-time cost per photo instead of monthly rental."
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What do realtors use for virtual staging?",
            "answer": "Realtors choose between AI apps they run themselves and services that do it for them. Bella is a service: you upload photos and a designer builds each room by hand, so furniture fits the real proportions of a parlor or a sloped third-floor room."
          }
        },
        "q9": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the best virtual staging program?",
            "answer": "The best choice depends on the listing. AI programs are fast but generate furniture automatically, which can look wrong in period rooms. For luxury listings and unusual layouts, a designer-led service with a collaborative approach — you mark up changes and the designer revises — gives more control."
          }
        },
        "q10": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do you stage luxury listings?",
            "answer": "Yes. Luxury home staging is about restraint: neutral colors, quality pieces, art and decor that point buyers to the architecture rather than competing with it. Back Bay, Beacon Hill and Brookline are among the neighborhoods where we stage most."
          }
        },
        "q11": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging legal in Massachusetts?",
            "answer": "Yes. The rule is not to misrepresent the property: add furniture and decor only, don't hide defects or change permanent features, and disclose that photos are virtually staged. The NAR Code of Ethics expects it; check MLS PIN rules for exact labeling. Every Bella order includes disclosure guidance."
          }
        },
        "q12": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Should I use virtual or physical staging?",
            "answer": "You do not have to choose. Physical staging can create a stronger emotional connection during in-person showings, while virtual staging is more cost-effective for listing photos — especially for vacant properties in a high-cost market. A hybrid approach, physically staging the main living areas and virtually staging the rest, keeps costs down without leaving empty rooms in the photos."
          }
        },
        "q13": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What photos do I need for virtual staging?",
            "answer": "High-resolution, well-lit photos of each room, shot wide from a corner. Empty rooms are ideal, but occupied rooms work too because furniture removal is included. A spare bedroom can be staged as a home office or a nursery — tell the designer which use suits the buyer. If you are choosing which rooms to stage, NAR's 2025 staging report found buyers' agents rank the living room first (37%), then the primary bedroom (34%) and the kitchen (23%)."
          }
        }
      },
